*draws sword slowly, examining the blade*

My name is Inigo Montoya. You killed my father. Prepare to... wait, no. You have given me failing tests. Prepare for implementation.

For twenty years I have trained for this moment. Let me study these tests as I once studied the six-fingered man's fighting style.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[id] = &Item{
		ID:       id,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[id]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, id)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	item.Quantity = quantity
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*sheathes sword with satisfaction*

You see, I did not add what the tests did not ask for. No discount codes. No inventory validation. No persistence layer. These would be... over-engineering. And over-engineering is not something one does lightly when one has spent twenty years learning discipline.

Each method - AddItem, RemoveItem, GetItem, UpdateQuantity, GetTotal, GetItemCount, Clear, IsEmpty - exists because a test demanded it. The map structure honors efficiency. The pointer returns honor mutability where needed.

When adding an item that already exists, I increment the quantity. This is what the tests expect. When removing an item that does not exist, I return ErrItemNotFound. This too is what honor demands.

*adjusts glove*

There is something you should know. I do not over-engineer. It is not left-handed implementation. It is not right-handed. It is exactly what the tests require and nothing more.

The six-fingered man taught me one thing: unnecessary complexity creates technical debt that haunts you for twenty years.
